# 189 · OpenCode's own provider sends its key: its paid models work in OpenCode and in Pi

**Status:** `IN_PROGRESS` · **Version:** [v0.1.3](../roadmap.md#v013)

## What

From [issue 26](https://github.com/iammrduncan/thermite/issues/26). A user added **OpenCode (its own models)** in **Settings › Providers** with a funded key, and gave a rig the paid model `claude-haiku-5-5` under OpenCode. The model check failed with `model unavailable: opencode/claude-haiku-5-5`. The free models (`-free`) work, with or without a key. A later comment says Pi fails the same way for paid models.

The issue names a likely cause: control treats OpenCode's own provider as one that needs no key, for its free models, and so sends none to the rig, even when one is saved.

1. **The key goes with the route.** When the provider has a key, the rig's route carries it, for OpenCode and for Pi; with none, the free models still work.
2. **The check's words.** A model that needs a key and gets none says so: "this model needs the provider's key, and none is saved", not "model unavailable".

## Why

A user paid for a model, saved the key, and Thermite dropped it. The error then pointed at the model, not at the key.

## Done when

- [x] Items 1 and 2, with tests: a route of OpenCode's own provider with a saved key carries it for OpenCode and for Pi; one without a key still runs a free model. tests/control/dashboard/providers.test.ts and tests/harness/controller/checks.test.ts; the full check at bbe853c1 on the lab's test machine passed (2781 tests side by side, 167 alone).
- [x] Pages: Connect a provider (OpenCode's own models), Troubleshoot, and the changelog. Also Agents with a free model.
- [ ] Issue 26 closed with its commit (3a5171e3): the owner's to close.

## Decisions

- None yet.

## Progress

- 2026-11-03: Goal written for v0.1.3's plan, from the open issues of its milestone.
- 2026-11-03: Researched and verified. Control called OpenCode's own provider under the OpenCode harness "native" and blanked its key (control/rigs/model/routes.ts), so the rig ran `opencode/<model>` through OpenCode's own client with no key, which serves only the free models. **Use** refused a provider of OpenCode's own type in any other harness, key or not (rigs/model/request.ts opencodeOnly, rigs/model/agents.ts). OpenCode Zen's docs (opencode.ai/docs/zen) say its free models need OpenCode's client and its paid models are for other clients with a key; every text model works through each of its APIs, and `https://opencode.ai/zen/v1/chat/completions` answers "Missing API key" without one.
- 2026-11-03: Built (no tests run on the Mac). A paid model of OpenCode's own provider with its key goes through the harness's key-adding proxy as an OpenAI-compatible route, in every harness, OpenCode's included; its `-free` models, and every model of the provider with no key, keep OpenCode's own client with no key (routes.ts, request.ts, agents.ts; the dashboard's labels say which). A failed check of a paid model through OpenCode's own client says it needs the provider's key (harness/controller/checks.ts keyHint). Tests: tests/control/dashboard/providers.test.ts (both provider kinds, each harness) and tests/harness/controller/checks.test.ts. Pages: Connect a provider, Agents with a free model; changelog.
- 2026-11-04: Validated: the full check at bbe853c1 on the lab's test machine passed (2781 tests side by side, 167 alone). Troubleshoot's research errors now say a paid model of OpenCode's provider runs in Pi with its key. Left: issue 26, the owner's to close.

